<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=ct-scan">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Medicine and health</p><h1 class="enc-title">CT scan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1972">1972</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Computed tomography, CAT scan, EMI scan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A computed tomography (CT) scan is a medical imaging technique in which a rotating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/x-ray">X-ray</a> tube and a row of detectors measure X-ray attenuation by different tissues from many angles, and a computer reconstructs cross-sectional images of the body. The mathematical theory goes back to the Radon transform of 1917. The first commercially viable scanner was invented by Godfrey Hounsfield between 1967 and 1972 at a research branch of EMI, so the first production machines were called EMI scanners. The 1979 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physiology or Medicine went jointly to Hounsfield and Allan MacLeod Cormack for it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ct-scan.jpg" alt="CT scan" width="330" height="323" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tomáš Vendiš · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moderní_výpočetní_tomografie_s_přímo_digitální_detekcí_rentgenového_záření.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Woolley believed the attendants in the royal tombs had gone willingly to their deaths; <b>CT scans</b> of surviving skulls show they were killed by blows to the head, perhaps from the spiked end of a copper axe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1967 to 1972</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>EMI research branch</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Godfrey Hounsfield · Allan MacLeod Cormack</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>William H. Oldendorf received a US patent in October 1963 for an apparatus to investigate selected areas of interior objects obscured by dense material.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/x-ray">X-rays<span>1895</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=ct-scan">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
